<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Wang Jingwei regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1940 CE – 1945 CE · capital Nanjing</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The collaborationist government that the veteran Kuomintang leader Wang Jingwei set up at Nanjing in 1940 under Japanese protection.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a>, a close associate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> and rival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiang-kai-shek">Chiang Kai-shek</a>, believed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> could not win the war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. He fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongqing">Chongqing</a> in December 1938 and, after negotiations with Japan, inaugurated a 'reorganised' National Government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> on 30 March 1940, absorbing the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Provisional Government of the Republic of China</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/reformed-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Reformed Government of the Republic of China</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime used the name, flag and symbols of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a>, claiming to be its legitimate continuation. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognised it in 1940, and in 1943 it declared war on <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> and took over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shanghai-international-settlement">Shanghai International Settlement</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shanghai-french-concession">Shanghai French Concession</a>. In practice Japanese armies controlled it, and Wang's officials, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-gongbo">Chen Gongbo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-fohai">Zhou Fohai</a>, had limited room for action.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jingwei">Wang Jingwei</a> died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in November 1944, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chen-gongbo">Chen Gongbo</a> succeeded him. The regime dissolved after Japan surrendered in August 1945. Its leaders were tried as traitors, and Chen Gongbo was executed in 1946.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wang-jingwei-regime.jpg" alt="Wang Jingwei regime" width="320" height="294"><figcaption>Image: SVG file created by User:Mononomic on en.wikipedia.org. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Republic_of_China-Nanjing_(Peace,_Anti-Communism,_National_Construction).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1940 CE – 1945 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nanjing</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Wang Jingwei, Chen Gongbo</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>China</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reorganized National Government of the Republic of China, Nanjing Nationalist Government (1940-1945)</dd></dl></aside></div>
